<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:windows">
<!--
    angr test_decompiling_abnormal_switch_case_case3 (memmove @ 0x18003c330,
    msvcr120.dll): an image-base-relative jump table whose bound guard is
    "unrolled" / duplicated across MULTIPLE dispatch predecessors.

    memmove dispatches the small-count (count <= 16) tail copy through an
    image-base-relative jump table:
        r9  = 0x180000000                     (image base, a computed lea)
        eax = *(uint32*)(0x18003c410 + r8*4)  (17-entry RVA table)
        rax = r9 + rax
        jmp  *rax                              (BRANCHIND -> the switch)
    The index r8 (count) is bounded by `cmp $0x10,%r8 ; jbe` at the function
    ENTRY, far from the jmp.  But the dispatch block at 0x18003c3fc is ALSO
    re-entered from the backward-copy path (`jmp 0x18003c3fc`), where r8 is
    bounded by a DIFFERENT guard (`and $0x7 ; test ; jne`).  So the BRANCHIND's
    parent block has TWO predecessors, each ending in its own bound CBRANCH and
    its own copy of the switch index; the two indices meet in a MULTIEQUAL.

    Stage model: S2 switch-model.  The basic guard walk
    (JumpBasic::analyzeGuards) bounds a table by backing up through
    SINGLE-predecessor blocks to the dominating guard.  With size_in() > 1 it
    short-circuits to checkUnrolledGuard, which kuna left as an empty SEAM stub;
    no bound was found, the normalized range stayed > maxtablesize, the basic
    model declined, and flow.rs truncated the indirect jump to a call
    (rendered as a computed (code *)() call).

    Fix surface = a default-off ArchOption (switchmultipred) that ports the
    upstream multi-predecessor unrolled-guard machinery
    (checkCommonCbranch + BlockBasic::findMultiequal + liftVerifyUnroll) into
    the checkUnrolledGuard SEAM, so the common guard is lifted onto the
    MULTIEQUAL output (the switch index), the table bounds to [0, 17), and the
    existing image-base-relative readonly-LOAD table model recovers the switch.

    Pass 1 (default, option off): assertion #1 proves the bug reproduces:
    the dispatch renders as a computed call through a function pointer.
    Pass 2 (option switchmultipred on): assertion #2 proves the multi-predecessor
    guard is lifted, the table is bounded, and the dispatch is recovered as a
    switch.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x18003c330">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x18003c330" name="memmove"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu memmove</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option switchmultipred on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="switchmultipred #1: multi-predecessor switch renders as a computed call (bug reproduced)" min="1" max="1">\(\*\(code \*\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="switchmultipred #2: switchmultipred on bounds the table and recovers the switch" min="1" max="1">switch\(</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="switchmultipred #3: switchmultipred on recovers the first table case (index 0)" min="1" max="1">case 0:</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="switchmultipred #4: switchmultipred on recovers the last table case (index 16)" min="1" max="1">case 0x10:</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="switchmultipred #5: the recovered switch has no default case (matches angr's 17-case-no-default)" min="0" max="0">default:</stringmatch>
